1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:05,550
Marina Barrage and the End of
Singapore's Water-Dependency Anxiety

2
00:00:05,550 --> 00:00:10,161
A 350-metre dam sits across the
mouth of the Marina Channel,

3
00:00:10,241 --> 00:00:13,367
nine steel gates and seven pumps standing

4
00:00:13,447 --> 00:00:17,200
between a freshwater
reservoir and the open sea.

5
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:21,821
It was finished in 2008, it
created Singapore's 15th reservoir,

6
00:00:21,901 --> 00:00:26,970
and today it is also the largest
one on the island — built not on the

7
00:00:27,050 --> 00:00:32,050
fringes of the city but directly
across the water from its skyline.

8
00:00:32,050 --> 00:00:35,196
Nine 30-metre, 5-metre-high crest gates

9
00:00:35,276 --> 00:00:38,750
span the channel, each weighing 70 tonnes.

10
00:00:38,750 --> 00:00:42,178
Seven drainage pumps, 28 tonnes apiece,

11
00:00:42,258 --> 00:00:47,025
can together move 280 cubic
metres of water a second.

12
00:00:47,025 --> 00:00:50,204
Under normal conditions
the gates stay shut,

13
00:00:50,284 --> 00:00:53,025
holding the reservoir level constant.

14
00:00:53,025 --> 00:00:56,589
When it rains hard at low
tide, the gates open and

15
00:00:56,669 --> 00:01:00,750
gravity does the work, flushing
storm runoff out to sea.

16
00:01:00,750 --> 00:01:04,801
When it rains hard at high tide,
the gates stay shut and the

17
00:01:04,881 --> 00:01:09,425
pumps take over, forcing the runoff
out against the incoming tide.

18
00:01:09,425 --> 00:01:13,866
The same mechanism that keeps the
reservoir fresh also functions

19
00:01:13,946 --> 00:01:18,175
as flood relief for low-lying
downtown districts — Chinatown,

20
00:01:18,255 --> 00:01:23,200
Jalan Besar, Geylang — that used to
flood regularly before it existed.

21
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:27,448
It is a dam, a floodgate and a
pumping station, built to look,

22
00:01:27,528 --> 00:01:31,925
from the top, like nothing more
than a long concrete promenade.

23
00:01:31,925 --> 00:01:35,845
For most of Singapore's history
as an independent state,

24
00:01:35,925 --> 00:01:39,425
its fresh water has crossed
a border to get here.

25
00:01:39,425 --> 00:01:42,627
During the Japanese
invasion in February 1942,

26
00:01:42,707 --> 00:01:47,192
retreating British forces demolished
the pipeline carrying water

27
00:01:47,272 --> 00:01:51,330
across the Causeway from Johor,
and Singapore's supply ran

28
00:01:51,410 --> 00:01:54,754
desperately short within
days — a demonstration,

29
00:01:54,834 --> 00:01:59,400
unplanned but total, of exactly
how exposed the arrangement was.

30
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:03,481
Water kept crossing that same
Causeway for decades afterward

31
00:02:03,561 --> 00:02:07,711
under two agreements: the Tebrau
and Scudai Rivers Agreement,

32
00:02:07,791 --> 00:02:12,219
signed the 2nd of October 1961,
entitling Singapore to draw water

33
00:02:12,299 --> 00:02:16,796
from those rivers in exchange for
returning a share of it treated;

34
00:02:16,876 --> 00:02:21,026
and the Johor River Agreement
of 1962, entitling Singapore to

35
00:02:21,106 --> 00:02:25,475
up to 250 million gallons of raw
water a day, on similar terms.

36
00:02:25,475 --> 00:02:28,473
The 1961 agreement expired in

37
00:02:28,553 --> 00:02:31,950
August 2011 and was not renewed.

38
00:02:31,950 --> 00:02:36,950
The 1962 agreement runs until 2061.

39
00:02:36,950 --> 00:02:41,650
The dependency was never
just a matter of engineering.

40
00:02:41,650 --> 00:02:46,026
Days after Singapore's separation
from Malaysia in August 1965,

41
00:02:46,106 --> 00:02:50,129
Malaysian Prime Minister Tunku
Abdul Rahman told reporters

42
00:02:50,209 --> 00:02:55,646
that if Singapore's foreign policy proved
prejudicial to Malaysia's interests,

43
00:02:55,726 --> 00:02:58,051
"we could always bring pressure to

44
00:02:58,131 --> 00:03:02,375
bear on them by threatening to
turn off the water in Johor."

45
00:03:02,375 --> 00:03:05,644
The threat was never carried
out, but Lee Kuan Yew

46
00:03:05,724 --> 00:03:09,195
later said it convinced him
that as long as Singapore

47
00:03:09,275 --> 00:03:12,946
depended entirely on Malaysia
for water, it would always

48
00:03:13,026 --> 00:03:16,375
be a satellite state, not
a fully independent one.

49
00:03:16,375 --> 00:03:20,578
Water security stopped being a
utilities question and became,

50
00:03:20,658 --> 00:03:24,800
in the government's own framing,
a question of sovereignty.

51
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:28,715
Lee Kuan Yew is credited with
first conceiving a barrage

52
00:03:28,795 --> 00:03:32,780
across the Marina and Kallang
basins as far back as 1987,

53
00:03:32,860 --> 00:03:37,203
two decades before it was built
— a sign of how long "engineer

54
00:03:37,283 --> 00:03:41,340
the dependency away" had been
sitting on the drawing board

55
00:03:41,420 --> 00:03:45,700
rather than arriving as a sudden
response to any one crisis.

56
00:03:45,700 --> 00:03:49,810
By 2008, at a public water
event, he put the underlying

57
00:03:49,890 --> 00:03:53,238
priority as plainly as
he ever did in office:

58
00:03:53,318 --> 00:03:58,575
"Every other policy has to bend at
the knees for our water survival."

59
00:03:58,575 --> 00:04:04,249
That priority took the eventual shape
of PUB's "Four National Taps" strategy:

60
00:04:04,329 --> 00:04:08,583
local catchment, imported
water, reclaimed water (NEWater,

61
00:04:08,663 --> 00:04:12,325
launched commercially in
2003), and desalination.

62
00:04:12,325 --> 00:04:15,747
Each tap could be expanded
or shrunk independently

63
00:04:15,827 --> 00:04:19,108
of the others, so no
single failure — technical,

64
00:04:19,188 --> 00:04:22,900
political or otherwise —
could leave the country dry.

65
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:26,222
Marina Barrage's contribution
was to the first tap:

66
00:04:26,302 --> 00:04:30,624
by damming off the mouth of the
Kallang Basin and Singapore River,

67
00:04:30,704 --> 00:04:34,559
it converted a stretch of the
city's own stormwater runoff,

68
00:04:34,639 --> 00:04:39,975
previously discharged straight into the
sea, into a stable freshwater catchment.

69
00:04:39,975 --> 00:04:44,108
Combined with the smaller
Punggol and Serangoon reservoirs,

70
00:04:44,188 --> 00:04:46,893
it added roughly a sixth of Singapore's

71
00:04:46,973 --> 00:04:50,400
total land area to the
island's water catchment.

72
00:04:50,400 --> 00:04:54,980
Construction ran from March 2005
to October 2008, at a cost of

73
00:04:55,060 --> 00:05:00,467
226 million Singapore dollars for the
barrage structure itself (a larger,

74
00:05:00,547 --> 00:05:04,225
roughly 3 billion Singapore
dollars transformation

75
00:05:04,305 --> 00:05:08,735
of the wider Marina Bay and
Kallang Basin area was under way

76
00:05:08,815 --> 00:05:13,475
around it at the same time, and
is often quoted alongside it).

77
00:05:13,475 --> 00:05:16,584
Once the gates closed for good, the tidal,

78
00:05:16,664 --> 00:05:20,912
brackish river mouth behind
them became Marina Reservoir:

79
00:05:20,992 --> 00:05:25,619
a 240-hectare freshwater body
with a 10,000-hectare catchment,

80
00:05:25,699 --> 00:05:29,875
its level held constant
regardless of the tide outside.

81
00:05:29,875 --> 00:05:33,618
A level reservoir also meant
the water could be used

82
00:05:33,698 --> 00:05:37,735
in ways a tidal river never
allowed: dragon boat racing,

83
00:05:37,815 --> 00:05:40,896
kayaking, the National
Day Parade fireworks

84
00:05:40,976 --> 00:05:44,725
reflected on flat water
instead of mud at low tide.

85
00:05:44,725 --> 00:05:48,029
None of that was the reason
the barrage was built,

86
00:05:48,109 --> 00:05:52,575
but it turned out to be one of the
more visible things it enabled.

87
00:05:52,575 --> 00:05:56,254
Ask most Singaporeans what
Marina Barrage is, and a fair

88
00:05:56,334 --> 00:06:00,214
number will describe a rooftop
lawn: a wide-open stretch of

89
00:06:00,294 --> 00:06:04,308
grass where families picnic,
kids fly kites, and couples come

90
00:06:04,388 --> 00:06:08,402
for the skyline view, all sitting
more or less directly above

91
00:06:08,482 --> 00:06:12,375
the gates and pump rooms doing
the actual work underneath.

92
00:06:12,375 --> 00:06:16,747
That's not a coincidence — the
rooftop lawn is landscaped over the

93
00:06:16,827 --> 00:06:21,266
pumping station on purpose, part
of the same brief that asked for a

94
00:06:21,346 --> 00:06:26,000
piece of water infrastructure that
would also double as public space.

95
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,611
Most people standing on it
are enjoying exactly what

96
00:06:29,691 --> 00:06:33,159
they think they're enjoying;
very few are thinking

97
00:06:33,239 --> 00:06:36,575
about crest gates or the
1962 agreement at all.

98
00:06:36,575 --> 00:06:39,868
I remember the first
time I actually saw it,

99
00:06:39,948 --> 00:06:43,475
on a visit back to
Singapore after years away.

100
00:06:43,475 --> 00:06:47,623
What struck me wasn't the lawn or
the view — it was a real sense of

101
00:06:47,703 --> 00:06:51,157
pride in the accomplishment
itself, and underneath that,

102
00:06:51,237 --> 00:06:56,079
something closer to reassurance: that
the government had both the will and the

103
00:06:56,159 --> 00:07:00,450
ability to end a decades-long
dependency, whatever it cost to do it.

104
00:07:00,450 --> 00:07:02,553
That's not a small thing to feel

105
00:07:02,633 --> 00:07:05,225
about a piece of civil infrastructure.

106
00:07:05,225 --> 00:07:08,340
It's also, quite
separately, one of the most

107
00:07:08,420 --> 00:07:11,325
beautiful spots in Singapore after dark.

108
00:07:11,325 --> 00:07:15,050
The crest gates and pump housings
light up along the water,

109
00:07:15,130 --> 00:07:18,145
the reservoir goes still
and black, and out past

110
00:07:18,225 --> 00:07:21,305
the barrage the anchorage
lights of ships waiting

111
00:07:21,385 --> 00:07:25,125
offshore sit on the horizon
like a second, dimmer skyline.

112
00:07:25,125 --> 00:07:28,329
I've been back a few times
since that first visit,

113
00:07:28,409 --> 00:07:31,825
and the night view is the
one that's stayed with me.

114
00:07:31,825 --> 00:07:35,754
Why it matters today: the
anxiety this post is named

115
00:07:35,834 --> 00:07:39,995
for hasn't fully disappeared
— the 1962 agreement still

116
00:07:40,075 --> 00:07:44,004
has 35 years left to run,
and the Four Taps strategy

117
00:07:44,084 --> 00:07:48,325
is itself an ongoing balancing
act, not a finished one.

118
00:07:48,325 --> 00:07:50,923
But the barrage is the moment that

119
00:07:51,003 --> 00:07:54,075
anxiety stopped being purely defensive.

120
00:07:54,075 --> 00:07:59,478
It's a rare case of a government's most
existential worry getting converted,

121
00:07:59,558 --> 00:08:01,858
in full public view, into a place

122
00:08:01,938 --> 00:08:06,050
people go on a Sunday afternoon
without a second thought.
